+
+### Reviewed execution sequence
+
+1. The independent reviewer checks the exact PR head and inventory, WotLK/shared-resource preservation, remaining 44-workload posture contract, unchanged 11-workload vulnerability cohort, 12-workload runtime publication contract and all eight runtime nodes. Require Repo Checks, Kubescape render/admission checks and publication, scanning and anonymous retrieval of the exact receiver digest before merge. Refresh ownership, claim/PV/CSI handle links, current Flux prune settings and restore/clone consumers before approving the irreversible merge. Unexpected resources or ownership drift stop the process.
+2. After separately authorized merge, normal Flux removes `apps-mop` and its resources using observed `prune=true` / default MirrorPrune. **MoP namespace/PVC deletion and its Delete-reclaim Longhorn deletion are part of that reconciliation.** No staged retention annotation is assumed to have been applied. Kubernetes PVC protection and CSI controllers handle in-use claims; do not bypass them. Cross-owner receiver RBAC and the SkyFire runtime binding also prune; owners reconcile independently and may retry during namespace termination. Verify convergence of all remaining owners, absence of both retired Flux children, no retired namespace resource inventory and no controller that can recreate either application. If namespace/CSI deletion stalls, stop for diagnosis without forcing finalizers.
+3. Verify all MoP workloads, CronJobs and dependants have disappeared; let controller garbage collection remove Pods, ReplicaSets, ControllerRevisions, EndpointSlices, Longhorn snapshots/replicas and the single dedicated RWX share manager. Verify the two exact MoP PV/Longhorn volume handles and native snapshot identities disappear. Do not directly delete replica files, shared Longhorn policies, attachment tickets or snapshots from other volumes. Confirm WotLK service endpoints and game connectivity, unchanged databases/PVCs, node-agent readiness on all eight nodes, and remaining vulnerability, posture, runtime and incident-publishing functionality. Existing unresolved production qualification is still reported as incomplete; intentional retirement cannot make missing remaining evidence pass.
+4. OpenBrew's two PVCs and namespace have live `prune=disabled` and are outside current desired state. After a fresh namespace API-object inventory, consumer/attachment/snapshot/restore/clone review and explicit destructive execution approval, delete **only** the two listed PVCs, one at a time. Use each reviewed UID and a fresh resourceVersion as Kubernetes DeleteOptions preconditions; let normal Delete-reclaim CSI controllers remove the matching PV/volume/snapshots. Then delete only Namespace `openbrew` with its reviewed UID and fresh resourceVersion. Confirm absence and no recreation after each step. Do not race-delete PVs or force finalizers. The receipt contains baseline UIDs, which are not timeless deletion authority.
+5. Refresh Longhorn's native remote backup inventory for exactly the four volume IDs and compare every Backup/BackupVolume UID, URL, label, target and owner. Check all cluster volumes and CSI snapshot contents for restore/clone references and require no in-flight backup/restore. Following independent review and explicit execution approval, remove each exact dedicated native Backup through Longhorn's supported native delete operation, one at a time with UID/resourceVersion preconditions. Verify remote deletion completion after each; remove the now-empty dedicated BackupVolume metadata through the supported native workflow. The general retention helper intentionally keeps a latest point and cannot implement full retirement. Never purge the bucket, directly delete shared blocks, invoke cluster-wide archive cleanup or force finalizers.
+6. Privately enumerate B2 file versions, hide markers, unfinished uploads, locks, replication and any separate copies under each reviewed dedicated Longhorn volume prefix. Native metadata does not prove all B2 versions disappeared. Use supported Longhorn cleanup first; review any remaining exact object-version IDs with the owner/B2 administrator before deleting only proven dedicated versions. Preserve the shared bucket, all unrelated backups, shared recovery credentials and every cluster SystemBackup. Shared archives may describe retired objects: exclude those objects from future restore; do not destroy unrelated archive history to erase a description.
+7. The NAS owner must prove exclusive ownership and absence of NAS/manual clients, symlinks, snapshots and other copies before removing only `192.168.1.154:/volume1/Plex Media/Games/MOP-5.4.8`. Cluster PV inspection found one PV for this path; that does not establish NAS-side exclusivity. Record actual bytes freed. Preserve the parent Games/Plex exports and every WotLK client/asset directory. Do not run a recursive deletion using a wildcard or an unverified mount path.
+8. Verify and remove only dedicated registry packages `ghcr.io/rovxbot/skyfire-mop` and `ghcr.io/rovxbot/openbrew-sqlserver`, including reviewed versions and build caches/artifacts after checking external consumers. Package API access was unavailable (403, missing read:packages). Do not remove upstream MySQL/SQL Server images, WotLK/registration packages or shared build caches. Remove authoritative DNS/Access configuration only for verified `openbrew.cooked.beer` record/application IDs; preserve the shared Cloudflare tunnel/zone/credentials. MoP has native LAN listeners on metal8 TCP 3724/8085, with no repository Tunnel/DNS entry; review any outside launcher/router/DNS configuration before removing only MoP rules. Preserve WotLK routing on its selected nodes.
+9. Reverify the private OpenBrew OIDC app/service-principal IDs and exclusive callback/grants; revoke only dedicated credentials and delete only that dedicated registration/SP. Remove dedicated password-manager/CI copies of application credentials, while retaining shared SOPS age keys, B2, Entra, registry and tunnel credentials. Review matching alert/incident retirement privately; do not call retirement a recovery, delete shared incident stores, expose private findings or close unrelated incidents. Publish Wiki completion only after observed rollout and separately reviewed disposal, recording actual resource recovery.
+
+For each manual Kubernetes deletion, construct `apiVersion: v1`, `kind: DeleteOptions`, with `preconditions.uid` and `preconditions.resourceVersion` from the freshly reviewed exact object. Submit to its **exact** namespaced API URL using `kubectl delete --raw URL -f DELETE_OPTIONS_FILE`; no selectors, `--all`, force, or stale identifiers. Stop on a precondition conflict, missing ownership evidence, changed target or incomplete controller cleanup. This is a reviewed procedure, not an instruction to execute during PR preparation.
+
+Prospective MoP reservation recovery is 2.50 CPU / 7.50 GiB RAM (2.25 CPU / 4.50 GiB on metal8, 0.25 CPU / 3 GiB on metal4); OpenBrew has no active pods and adds no current reservation saving. Storage is 115 GiB logical / 345 GiB nominal at three replicas; summed Longhorn actualSize implies approximately 35.72 GiB replicated allocation, not a measured disk-free result. NFS and B2 bytes remain unmeasured. MoP's share manager has no configured requests. No N-1 or replacement-workload capacity acceptance is inferred.
+
+There is no data rollback promise after PVC/native-backup/NFS/credential/package disposal. Before an authorized merge, amend the PR on unexpected state. After deletion begins, stop and diagnose or forward-fix through a reviewed change; do not redeploy old manifests or replay shared archives blindly.
